Step 1 — Write deployment_validation.sh
cd ~/2026b-cosc2767-a1-<repo-name>
git checkout develop
mkdir -p question-7
nano question-7/deployment_validation.sh

#!/bin/bash

URL="http://<Tomcat-Server-IP>:9xxx/mywebsite-<student-id>/"
EXPECTED_TEXT="Welcome to [name]'s Web App"

PAGE_CONTENT=$(curl -s "$URL")

if echo "$PAGE_CONTENT" | grep -q "$EXPECTED_TEXT"; then
  echo "Validation SUCCESS: Expected text found at $URL"
  exit 0
else
  echo "Validation FAILURE: Expected text NOT found at $URL"
  exit 1
fi

Replace <Tomcat-Server-IP> with EC2 public IP, 9xxx with actual port from Question 5, <student-id> with WAR's actual name, and EXPECTED_TEXT with the exact text currently in index.jsp from Question 4 (must match verbatim, or grep won't find it).

curl -s = silent mode (no progress bar cluttering the output).
grep -q = quiet mode, no output, just a pass/fail exit code internally — if reacts to that.
exit 0 = success (the Unix convention: 0 means "no error"), exit 1 = failure — this exit code is exactly what Jenkins reads to decide the build's overall status.

chmod +x question-7/deployment_validation.sh
git add question-7/deployment_validation.sh
git commit -m "Add deployment validation script"
git push
Step 2 — Add the post-build shell step in Jenkins

Go to devops-ci-<student-id> job → Configure → scroll to Build Steps (below the Maven build step) → Add build step → Execute shell:

# Deploy the freshly-built WAR to Tomcat
cp $WORKSPACE/question-4/my_project/target/*.war /opt/tomcat/webapps/

# Give Tomcat a moment to unpack and start serving it
sleep 10

# Run the validation script — its exit code decides this build's pass/fail
bash $WORKSPACE/question-7/deployment_validation.sh
$WORKSPACE is a Jenkins-provided variable pointing at the exact folder the repo was cloned into for this build — using it instead of a hardcoded path keeps this portable.
The sleep 10 matters: Tomcat needs a few seconds to actually unpack and deploy the WAR after the file lands in webapps/; without it, curl might hit the URL before the app is actually live and fail with a false negative.
Because this shell step is the last step, its exit code (from the validation script, since it's the last command run) becomes the build's overall result — no extra Jenkins config needed for this wiring, it's automatic.

Save.

